Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: gridline-session
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         hyprland,
         xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland,
         wayland-utils,
         xwayland,
         swaylock,
         swayidle,
         hyprpolkitagent,
         xdg-terminal-exec,
         kitty,
         waybar,
         mako-notifier,
         hyprpaper,
         fuzzel,
         libnotify-bin,
         ubuntu-wallpapers
Description: Gridline session integration
 Gridline is an optional Wayland tiling desktop session for Ubuntu. This
 package owns the session entry the display manager reads, the session
 launcher, and the compositor adapter layer.
 .
 Selecting Gridline at the login screen runs the launcher, which asks the
 active adapter what the compositor provides and starts the session only when
 every required capability is reported. A missing one ends the launch with its
 name in the system journal and returns the user to the login screen, where
 the stock Ubuntu session stays selectable.
 .
 The session refuses to start without the components it records as blocking:
 the compositor, its portal backend, the Wayland registry query the adapter
 asks through, the screen locker and the polkit authentication agent. A desktop
 that cannot lock must not run, and privileged actions that fail with no prompt
 are worse than a session that declines.
 .
 The polkit agent is a systemd user unit the archive ships disabled, so this
 package also owns a user target that wants it and that pulls in
 graphical-session.target, which no session may start directly. Gridline wants
 the unit in its own target rather than enabling it, so installing Gridline
 changes nothing about a GNOME session on the same machine.
 .
 This package also depends on a status bar, a notification daemon and a
 wallpaper daemon, so that selecting Gridline presents a desktop rather than a
 bare compositor. None of the three is blocking: each one's absence degrades
 the desktop and leaves the session usable, because a desktop that shows less
 is worse and not unsafe.
 .
 Every package this one depends on is in the universe component except
 xwayland, xdg-terminal-exec and ubuntu-wallpapers, which are in main. The
 last of those is depended on for one file: the symlink it maintains at the
 release's own wallpaper, which the shipped wallpaper configuration names so
 that a per-release rename does not leave the desktop blank. Universe carries no
 Canonical security-update commitment. Main does. Patching them is the
 operator's responsibility, which on a personal machine means its owner.

Package: gridline-config
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         xdg-desktop-portal,
         xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
Description: Gridline configuration defaults and policy input
 Gridline is an optional Wayland tiling desktop session for Ubuntu. This
 package owns the shipped defaults: the compositor configuration and its
 keybindings, the configuration for the status bar, the notification daemon,
 the wallpaper daemon and the application launcher, the terminal the session
 opens, and the portal backend mapping.
 .
 Everything here is a default a user can decline. Replacing any of these files
 with your own, in the place the component itself searches, makes the session
 use yours and stop naming Gridline's.
 .
 It will also own the administrator's policy input, the lock declarations and
 the destination of the generated configuration, which are not built yet. The
 directories keeping administrator-authored configuration separate from
 generated output are established from the start.

Package: gridline-tools
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Gridline command-line tools
 Gridline is an optional Wayland tiling desktop session for Ubuntu. This
 package owns the gridline command. Its one subcommand is doctor, which
 reports each component a Gridline session expects, whether it is running,
 when that was last observed, and what its absence costs.
 .
 doctor answers from what the session wrote down rather than by inspecting
 the system, so it still reports when no session is running, which is the
 case a user most needs it in.
 .
 It will also own the theme commands and the generator that produces the
 effective configuration, which are not built yet.
